INT: What happened in your house after he died?

HB: I have to quit school. My older sister has to go to work. And I got a part-time job, because I was not 18 years old yet when my father died.

So I worked in a little factory. It was connected with bells-- doorbells. Of course, it's different than it is now. Now you have the batteries. And [INAUDIBLE] at this time you have to wire little, like, little cables for these bells. And my daughter worked-- my sister-- the oldest sister worked in a factory sardine. She packed the sardines in the-- the cans. Yeah.

INT: When-- when did things start to get bad for you? When-- when did the Germans start instituting antisemitic laws?

HB: When the Germans invaded Poland, part of Poland was taken by the Russians. And the border. [INAUDIBLE] And the rest was taken by the Germans. And we was without a country.

INT: How did that affect you?

HB: Affect that very much.

INT: Please describe that.

HB: First of all, all the equipment, what my father got, it was very expensive. Some of them he ordered in German, in Leipzig, [? cause ?] on Polish Lips-- Lips-- Leipzig. Leipzig. Yeah. We have to give away, of course. That's the first thing. They took away, the Germans. It was very expensive equipment-- the finest.

INT: Describe how they took it away.

HB: Well, they asked through the speakers [INAUDIBLE] the streets speakers, whoever has any sewing machines-- of course, expensive-- not old one-- equipment for like doctors, like dentists should give away for the German army. If you want to avoid that to take you to Germany for-- for a work, to work for Germans, you have to give it away. And then, when the first little ghetto was, and we have to move out from there--

INT: I'd like to just stop for one moment and go back. How did you feel? What did your mother say when she heard these loudspeakers announcing that you had to give up your machinery?

HB: Well, she said, because they're-- they said you're going to avoid taking you to work, to force you to work for the Germans, we better give them away. That didn't help.

INT: Were there any other antisemitic actions taken?

HB: A lot of in our area, we was rather-- we lived very good together, like neighbors. There-- a lot of them, there were sorry what the German does to us. But they took a lot of Polacks, Catholics. Also took to-- to work for the Germans.

They forced them to work, like children, 14, 15 years. They took them to Germany right away to work for the farmers. So they were not friendly to the Germans. Some was happy that we have to go out from the apartment to-- they ask [? her ?] to leave because that's not going-- be going to live with the Jews only.

INT: I just want to clarify. Who are you talking about when you talk about 'they'?

HB: I'm talking about the Catholic people.

INT: Please continue describing what-- what the Germans ordered you to do.

HB: First of all, they closed all the stores-- the Jewish stores-- the grocery stores. There was a lot-- a lot-- not big, small grocery stores. They have to close. They used to buy by the farmers. They're not allowed to go there more. So they have to close. So we felt right away that we don't have stores to buy the groceries.

There was a one woman. She was a widow. And she got three cows. And she sold the cow-- the milk around. She-- she made cheeses she sold. And everyone was buying because it was fresh. They took away the cows, because the-- the Germans need the cow-- need the meat.

INT: And this woman, was she Jewish?

HB: Yes, Jewish. Jewish. Yeah.

INT: Who was taking the things away from the Jews? Was it the Germans, or was it the Polish people?

HB: The Germans asked the Polish people to take-- to help them. Some they took themselves. Some Polish people, they have to. They was afraid. They have to.

Some didn't want to do it. So there was-- they went hiding. There-- they didn't do that.

INT: Who went hiding?

HB: Most of the people, like motorist from the trains, tram, tram, street trains. They was very good to us.

INT: So are these Jewish motorists, or are these Polish motorists?

HB: Polish Catholics. A lot of people-- I mean, the hard believers-- the Catholic hard believers-- they said, God is for everyone. If-- if we help each other, God will help us.

Some of them helped us, especial when-- when we have to leave the-- the apartment, and the furniture. We're not allowed to take them. Nothing. Just some pots, dishes, clothing. Whatever you could take it. But you're not-- you couldn't get somebody to move you, because you're not allowed to take the furnitures.

INT: Could you describe that a little bit more, when the order came to leave your apartment?

HB: Yes. The one day-- one day they make flyers, and it was hanging all over in German language and in Polish language that everyone from this area has to move out. And they didn't tell you where that you'd get an apartment. No. So we have to move, too.

The first was [NON-ENGLISH] Street. That was the little ghetto. But it was not closed this time. So we find somebody who took us in.

It was a big apartment-- five rooms. So we got two rooms. Two rooms, and we used the kitchen and the other, the toilet and that. And that, when the-- that-- that street tram you call that-- street tram-- used to go through the ghetto, for the little ghetto, and out to the Aryan side, you know.

And we also got the court house on [NON-ENGLISH] Street, which I remember because I used it. It was two entrances. One was from this side from the ghetto. The other was from the Aryan street.

You know, a lot of Jewish people, they got friend. They got co-workers. They worked together. They had their relatives, mixed marriages, relatives. They got the colleagues from-- colleagues from-- from some schools, from universities.

So we exchanged a lot of things for food, you know, what because right away we got different money in the ghetto. We couldn't buy for this money out of the ghetto nothing, because nobody would take that. So they make special money for the ghetto.

INT: Could you describe that a little bit more?

HB: It the-- in the short-- the small ghetto was a short time.

INT: When was that?

HB: Only about-- about not even a year. And then they start building the [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] the fences. Not fences. How do you call that? Brick walls in the ghetto. And then they closed the ghetto.

INT: Before we get to the big ghetto, I'd like to go back to the small ghetto. When was that that it started?

HB: That was the end of '41 and the beginning of '42.

