And my mother was crying, please don't go. Because when they find out that you're Jewish, they're going to kill you. That what they did. If somebody went out, I have to take out of the-- the star, what I wear in the ghetto, you know? Because I couldn't wear out the side-- the ghetto, the star.

And one time, I went to-- in [POLISH] was a store, a Polish store. And I was so hungry this time. We didn't have bread for about four or five days, nothing, nothing to eat. So I stole a bread, a loaf of bread. And the owner caught me on that. And he says, I'm going to report you to the police. You from the ghetto.

I say, yes. So he looked at me. He says, you know what? I'm a Catholic. If you would steal pastry or candies, I would report you to the police. But I can't report you because you took only a bread. Take the bread and never come back because I am in danger when they see you in my store.

So I was happy with the bread. And I was happy he didn't report me to the police. The worst people in the Polish population was the Polish police, because they worked with the Germans. They helped them. If they recognized a Jew, they would report them. They would take him to the Gestapo.

INT: Did you ever see any Jews being reported to the Gestapo?

HB: Oh, yes.

INT: Could you tell me about that?

HB: I saw two brothers. The one was maybe 14 years old. The other was maybe 16 years old. And the policeman caught them both. They was-- they got potatoes all over here, hidden, you know? And the policeman was driving a-- a bicycle. And he saw them. They was so skinny, dirty, you know?

And that, I saw that. I was hiding behind-- behind a-- a building. And he took them to the Gestapo. I don't know what happened to them. I didn't think they were-- they let them out alive. I don't think so. That was the worst one.

INT: Could you describe in a little bit more detail what the streets of the ghettos looked like?

HB: [SIGHS] How can you describe people dying on the streets from hunger, children? They didn't know what is war. They want to eat. How can you describe that, that every morning, the company, the burial company-- [? Pinkert ?] was the name of the company-- every morning, they came with a wagon with a horse, a wagon with a horse, and collect the bodies-- children, woman, elderly, young. They collect them and threw them in the wagon like pieces of rocks. The legs, the hand, was shaking from them.

[PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

How can you describe newborn children they killed? I was-- one time, I asked my mother. I came home from the bakery. And I said, Mama, you tell us always to believe in God. How can God look at that and do nothing to the Germans, just kill us? And they have the belts on them, written, [? 'Gott wit uns,' ?] which means, God with us.

