[PAUSES FOR 8 SECONDS]

I don't know how to describe you that the Germans announce to the speakers that the Jews has to give away gold, silver, dollars if they don't want to go to the concentration camps. So we give away whatever we got. And when there was no more gold, no more nothing, the Germans said to the congregation head of the-- the Jew, Mr. Czerniaków, if you won't deliver gold, you have to deliver every day 500 Jews to the [GERMAN]. So he committed suicide.

INT: Could we go back just a moment? You're talking about this man who committed suicide. What was his name?

HB: Czerniaków.

INT: Czerniaków-- could you tell me a little bit more? What was Czerniaków's role?

HB: First of all, he was like the advocate between the Jews and the Germans. Of course, he has to do whatever the Germans ask him to do. Like one week, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] they announced to the speakers that it was in the winter, that the German soldiers are freezing on the front, you know, fighting. Whoever have fur coats, fur hats, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] new socks, warm sweaters, we have to deliver that.

But to tell you the truth, whoever has something, exchanged for bread. It was on the street, you would see thousands of people. They have a little of everything from clothing. Soap was like a piece of gold, because we didn't get any soap to wash.

I was sick of typhus in the ghetto. And there was a quarantine. And we didn't get even food there, nothing.

INT: There's many things I want to ask you about. First, could you-- Czerniaków was the head of the Judenrat?

HB: Yes. Yes.

INT: So he was Jewish?

HB: He was Jewish, Czerniaków, yeah.

