INT: What was your reaction?

HB: I believed him. I believed him. Because it was a time when the Jews, they give away a lot of things, like gold and diamonds, to avoid to concentration camp. For about a week or two, they didn't take any Jews. So we believed it. Then after that, they ask for more and more.

Maybe some people was-- I'm sure was hiding something for-- for a rainy day, for a hard time, you know? But a lot of people-- I just-- I'm just asking, where is this gold they took from the ghetto, not only from the Warsaw ghetto? They took from Lódz, from the big ghetto. They took from other big cities, from Kraków. There was a ghetto. Everyone has to give away whatever they-- they wanted.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

So he was in a very bad spot, Mr. Czerniaków. And then-- and he didn't wanted to give to deliver the people to the [GERMAN]. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] So he committed suicide.

INT: And what was your reaction when he did that?

HB: I consider him a hero, because he could easily do that. There was the Jewish police. He could ask them to deliver the people to the [GERMAN].

INT: Could you describe how you found out that he committed suicide?

HB: Everyone knew in the ghetto, everyone.

INT: How?

HB: They said in the-- through the speakers. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] There was somebody who was replacing him, you know? And he announced it after he committed suicide.

INT: Do you remember who replaced him?

HB: No. No. That was the whole [? commit-- ?] a whole executive or how you call that.

[DRINKS]

INT: What did you understand to be happening outside of the Warsaw ghetto?

