[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

One of-- one group of them helped them for money, for gold, for diamonds, for that. The other group helped for just humanity. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I know one family who got killed, the children, all of them. He was a socialist.

And he was hiding two little girls. And some-- somebody went to the police or the Germans and told that he was hiding two Jewish children. Of course, they killed the children. They killed the whole Polish family.

That was one group of people. The other was people who wouldn't hurt you, but they was afraid to help. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Sometimes they give you a piece of bread and said, don't come here more. Because I'm afraid for my family, which I don't blame them.

The worst was the third one who worked with the police and with the Germans. If they-- let's say a Polack was-- looks like a Jew. And he looks suspicious to them. They would ask him to go in and show if he is Jewish. You know how. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] A lot of people get killed this way, a lot of Jews.

[PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

That was three kind-- types of Polish people. I have to say that I didn't have gold and money, nothing. Of course, a lot of times, they said, I can't give you nothing. I'm afraid. Go away. But they didn't report me.

But a lot of times, I got help, like I couldn't stay in the apartment sometimes one night. But the people were afraid. But they give me food. They feed me, give me food. And they say go, which was a lot when you hungry, you know, and when I have to take the food and go into the ghetto for my family, you know? There was no men in the family.

